Method : Method is a block of code that performs a specific task and
         can be reused whenever needed.
A method:
-->Has a name
-->May take inputs (parameters)
-->Executes some statements (code)
-->May return a value
-->Inside a method you cannot write another method

SYNTAX :
access_modifier return_type method_name(parameters) {
    // method body (code)
}

Access Modifier
Defines who can use the method
Examples:
public → accessible everywhere
private → only inside the class
protected → within package + subclasses

Return Type
Type of value the method returns
Examples:
int → returns integer
double → returns decimal
void → returns nothing

Method Name
Name of the method (e.g., addNumbers, display)
Should follow naming rules (camelCase)

Parameters
Inputs to the method
Written inside ()
Example: (int a, int b)
Can be empty → ()
Method Body
Code inside { }
Executes when method is called

Example 1 (No return, no parameters) :
public static void greet() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}

Example 2 (With parameters & return) :
public static int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

Methods can be:

Void / Non-void
With / Without parameters
Static / Non-static

Void Method (no return value)
Does not return anything
public static void greet() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}

Non-Void Method (returns value)
Returns a value using return
public static int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

Static Method
Called using class name
static void hello() {
    System.out.println("Hi");
}

Non-Static Method
void show() {
    System.out.println("Instance method");
}

No parameters + No return value
public static void greet() {
    System.out.println("Hello");
}

No parameters + Return value
public static int getNumber() {
    return 10;
}

Parameters + No return value
public static void printSum(int a, int b) {
    System.out.println(a + b);
}

Parameters + Return value (Most important)
public static int multiply(int a, int b) {
    return a * b;
}

Simples codes to understand Methods using :

1.Void vs Non-Void Methods

public class MethodTypes1 {

    // Void method (no return)
    static void greet() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    // Non-void method (returns value)
    static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        greet();  // calling void method

        int result = add(5, 3);  // calling non-void
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

2. With Parameters vs Without Parameters

public class MethodTypes2 {

    // Without parameters
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("No parameters");
    }

    // With parameters
    static void display(int num) {
        System.out.println("Number: " + num);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        show();           // no input
        display(10);      // passing input
    }
}

3. Static vs Non-Static Methods

public class MethodTypes3 {

    // Static method
    static void staticMethod() {
        System.out.println("Static method");
    }

    // Non-static method
    void nonStaticMethod() {
        System.out.println("Non-static method");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // Calling static method
        staticMethod();

        // Calling non-static method
        MethodTypes3 obj = new MethodTypes3();
        obj.nonStaticMethod();
    }
}




